gsnaligndesign:src-0001
    a gsnaligndesign:CitedPassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsnaligndesign:argo-59dd512f ;
    gsnaligndesign:locator "argo.ttl:143-146" ;
    gsnaligndesign:passageText """:isSupportedBy rdf:type owl:ObjectProperty ;
               owl:inverseOf :supports ;
               rdfs:label "is supported by"@en .""" .

gsnaligndesign:src-0002
    a gsnaligndesign:CitedPassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsnaligndesign:argo-59dd512f ;
    gsnaligndesign:locator "argo.ttl:172-174" ;
    gsnaligndesign:passageText """:opposes rdf:type owl:ObjectProperty ;
         rdfs:label "opposes"@en .""" .

gsnaligndesign:src-0003
    a gsnaligndesign:CitedPassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsnaligndesign:ArgOAxioms ;
    gsnaligndesign:locator "search for 'opposes', 'undermines', 'contradicts', 'negates', 'isSupportedBy'" ;
    gsnaligndesign:passageText "(no occurrences - the opposition family and the support family are absent from ARGO's first-order axiomatisation entirely)" .

gsnaligndesign:src-0004
    a gsnaligndesign:CitedPassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsnaligndesign:argo-59dd512f ;
    gsnaligndesign:locator "argo.ttl:301-308" ;
    gsnaligndesign:passageText """[ rdf:type owl:Restriction ;
  owl:onProperty :hasConclusion ;
  owl:qualifiedCardinality "1"^^xsd:nonNegativeInteger ;
  owl:onClass :sentencecontent ] ;
skos:definition "An information content entity that has at least one premise or supposition and exactly one conclusion."@en""" .

gsnaligndesign:src-0005
    a gsnaligndesign:CitedPassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsnaligndesign:locator "ontogsn.ttl:698-699, quoting the standard" ;
    gsnaligndesign:passageText "A GSN Goal that presents the pinnacle claim in an argument. It is 'top' in terms of the argument hierarchy, rather than necessarily its physical layout. There may be more than one top goal in a GSN structure." .

gsnaligndesign:src-0006
    a gsnaligndesign:CitedPassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsnaligndesign:argo-59dd512f ;
    gsnaligndesign:locator "argo.ttl:232-239" ;
    gsnaligndesign:passageText "An argument with at least one argument as a proper continuant part, and which has only arguments as continuant parts. [minted at http://www.github/argumentsontology#complexargument - hash, not slash]" .

gsnaligndesign:src-0007
    a gsnaligndesign:CitedPassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsnaligndesign:argo-59dd512f ;
    gsnaligndesign:locator "argo.ttl:371-375" ;
    gsnaligndesign:passageText "A sentence content that is affirmed or accepted input to and output of distinct acts of inferring in distinct arguments that are proper continuant parts of a complex argument." .

gsnaligndesign:src-0008
    a gsnaligndesign:CitedPassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsnaligndesign:argo-59dd512f ;
    gsnaligndesign:locator "argo.ttl:349-358" ;
    gsnaligndesign:passageText """An information content entity representing the propositional meaning of a sentence.
Examples: "2+2 =5", "Close the door!", "Is the present King of France bald?", "The man walked into the bar." """ .

gsnaligndesign:src-0009
    a gsnaligndesign:CitedPassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsnaligndesign:argo-59dd512f ;
    gsnaligndesign:locator "argo.ttl:327-332, 274-280" ;
    gsnaligndesign:passageText """:languageact rdfs:subClassOf <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/BFO_0000015> ;  # process
             skos:definition "A process involving the intentional use of language."@en .
:actofinferring rdfs:subClassOf :languageact .""" .

gsnaligndesign:src-0010
    a gsnaligndesign:CitedPassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsnaligndesign:locator "ontogsn.ttl:1174, quoting the standard" ;
    gsnaligndesign:passageText "GSN solutions make no claim, but are simply references to evidence items that provide support for a particular claim. They shall therefore be stated as noun-phrases." .

gsnaligndesign:src-0011
    a gsnaligndesign:CitedPassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsnaligndesign:argo-59dd512f ;
    gsnaligndesign:locator "argo.ttl:335-339, 251-256" ;
    gsnaligndesign:passageText """:premise skos:definition "A sentence content that is affirmed input to an act of inferring."@en .
:actofaffirming skos:definition "A language act in which an agent believes a sentence content is either true or false based on evidence and within rationality constraints."@en .""" .

gsnaligndesign:src-0012
    a gsnaligndesign:CitedPassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsnaligndesign:argo-59dd512f ;
    gsnaligndesign:locator "argo.ttl:378-382, 210-218" ;
    gsnaligndesign:passageText """:supposition skos:definition "A sentence content that is accepted input to an act of inferring."@en .
:suppositionIn skos:definition "A relation between a sentence content and an argument, where the sentence content is accepted but not affirmed and serves as input to an act of inferring within the argument."@en .""" .

gsnaligndesign:src-0013
    a gsnaligndesign:CitedPassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsnaligndesign:ArgOPaper ;
    gsnaligndesign:locator "section 'Extensions: Argument Schemes, Truth, Probability, Validity'" ;
    gsnaligndesign:passageText "We therefore advise that ArgO may accommodate evaluations of sentence contents by adding a subclass of the BFO class Process, which we here call Act of Evaluation. [...] evaluations of statements are treated as information content entities, rather than as qualities of statements carrying unintuitive ontological commitments." .

gsnaligndesign:src-0014
    a gsnaligndesign:CitedPassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsnaligndesign:argo-59dd512f ;
    gsnaligndesign:locator "argo.ttl:49-57" ;
    gsnaligndesign:passageText """:conclusionIn rdf:type owl:ObjectProperty ;
              owl:inverseOf :hasConclusion ;
              rdf:type owl:FunctionalProperty ;
              rdfs:domain :sentencecontent ;
              rdfs:range :argument .""" .

gsnaligndesign:src-0015
    a gsnaligndesign:CitedPassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsnaligndesign:ArgOAxioms ;
    gsnaligndesign:locator "section 'CLASS CONSTRAINT AXIOMS'" ;
    gsnaligndesign:passageText """all x all y all t((ComplexArgument(y) & conclusionIn(x,y,t))-> exists z(Argument(z) & properContinuantPartOfAt(z,y,t) & conclusionIn(x,z,t))).
%The conclusion of a complex argument must be the conclusion of one of the complex arguments's proper parts""" .

gsnaligndesign:src-0016
    a gsnaligndesign:CitedPassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsnaligndesign:ArgOPaper ;
    gsnaligndesign:locator "section 'ArgO: An Arguments Ontology'" ;
    gsnaligndesign:passageText "ArgO [is] a small ontology of arguments designed to meet the constraints we have described and to be readily adopted and extended by others." .

gsnaligndesign:src-0017
    a gsnaligndesign:CitedPassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsnaligndesign:locator "ontogsn.ttl:1197, quoting the standard" ;
    gsnaligndesign:passageText "A strategy, rendered as a parallelogram, describes the inference that exists between a goal and its supporting goal(s)." .


#################################################################
#    Rationales
#################################################################

gsnaligndesign:why-0001 a gsndesign:Rationale ; gsndesign:rationaleText
    "An alignment is only valid against the versions it was written for. ARGO is a development release and OntoGSN is pre-1.3, so either can move a term underneath this graph. Recording both version strings on the ontology header makes the scope of every axiom below explicit, and makes staleness detectable by string comparison rather than by re-deriving the analysis."@en .

gsnaligndesign:why-0002 a gsndesign:Rationale ; gsndesign:rationaleText
    "The target ARGO property carries no domain, no range, no characteristics and no definition, and appears nowhere in ARGO's first-order axiomatisation. It is a placeholder its authors left to be extended. A sub-property axiom into it therefore entails nothing and cannot contradict anything, which makes it the cheapest honest way to state a correspondence."@en .

gsnaligndesign:why-0003 a gsndesign:Rationale ; gsndesign:rationaleText
    "OntoGSN is a faithful rendering of the GSN Community Standard v3 and is the source of truth for it. An axiom whose subject is a gsn: term still belongs in the alignment graph, because it is a claim about ARGO rather than a claim the standard makes. Keeping it here also means a GSN-only consumer never loads BFO."@en .

gsnaligndesign:why-0004 a gsndesign:Rationale ; gsndesign:rationaleText
    "SKOS mapping properties do not entail, which is the point: the correspondence is recorded for a reader without committing a reasoner. Their rdfs:domain is skos:Concept, so applying them to OWL classes puns those classes as individuals; harmless in OWL 2 DL, and contained by keeping the axioms out of both aligned ontologies."@en .

gsnaligndesign:why-0005 a gsndesign:Rationale ; gsndesign:rationaleText
    "Extensionally the GSN class is the broader of the two. A goal structure with two top goals corresponds to two ARGO complex arguments, one per top goal, each omitting the other; where the two subtrees share elements the arguments overlap, which ARGO permits since its arguments are wholes with parts rather than disjoint aggregates. closeMatch is the conservative reading and avoids asserting a direction the extensions do not clearly support."@en .

gsnaligndesign:why-0006 a gsndesign:Rationale ; gsndesign:rationaleText
    "A goal carries a statement; it is not one. The goal is a node with an identifier, decorators and a string, while an ARGO statement is the meaning that string expresses. relatedMatch is the weakest available predicate and is the honest one here; the load is carried by gsnalign:expressesContent."@en .

gsnaligndesign:why-0007 a gsndesign:Rationale ; gsndesign:rationaleText
    "The closest pair in the two ontologies, and still not an equality. An ARGO supposition is an input to an act of inferring and is typically discharged; a GSN assumption hangs off a contextual relation and is never discharged. The mapping holds only if contextual inputs are taken to feed the inference - a commitment GSN does not make. Adopting the ARGO view is stricter than GSN requires, which is acceptable: the strictness is opt-in with the alignment."@en .

gsnaligndesign:why-0008 a gsndesign:Rationale ; gsndesign:rationaleText
    "A strategy is a continuant that describes reasoning; an act of inferring is the reasoning, an occurrent. BFO makes the two disjoint, so the axiom adds nothing a reasoner could not derive. It is asserted anyway because equating them is the first mapping most readers reach for, and an explicit disjointness is a better warning than a comment."@en .

gsnaligndesign:why-0009 a gsndesign:Rationale ; gsndesign:rationaleText
    "A GSN inference step exists only as an implicit fan-in - a parent goal and whatever supports it - with no individual to carry it. ARGO's premise and conclusion relations need one. Minting per parent goal rather than per relationship is forced by arity: a relationship is a single edge, and a step with three supporting goals needs one individual holding three premises."@en .

gsnaligndesign:why-0010 a gsndesign:Rationale ; gsndesign:rationaleText
    "Anchoring the complex argument on the top goal rather than on the gsn:Argument satisfies ARGO's exactly-one-conclusion requirement by construction. Anchoring on the gsn:Argument would collide with it whenever a structure has more than one top goal, and the collision is silent: with no explicit difference between the two conclusions, a reasoner merges them rather than reporting an error."@en .

gsnaligndesign:why-0011 a gsndesign:Rationale ; gsndesign:rationaleText
    "Content is keyed on the statement string rather than on the element. GSN away elements repeat a claim from another module as a separate individual, whereas ARGO sentence contents are generically dependent continuants and are identical across bearers. Keying on the string is what makes the two identity criteria agree."@en .

gsnaligndesign:why-0012 a gsndesign:Rationale ; gsndesign:rationaleText
    "ARGO declares conclusionIn functional while its own first-order axiomatisation requires the conclusion of a complex argument to also be the conclusion of one of its proper parts. Asserting a conclusion at both the step and the case level would therefore force the two arguments to be identical. Asserting it at one level only sidesteps a defect in ARGO without depending on it being fixed."@en .

gsnaligndesign:why-0013 a gsndesign:Rationale ; gsndesign:rationaleText
    "The route from a GSN node to its ARGO counterpart cannot run through content: content is shared by every element with the same statement text, so the path is ambiguous wherever two goals are worded identically. An explicit functional link from the minted individual back to the goal it came from is the only unambiguous anchor."@en .

gsnaligndesign:why-0014 a gsndesign:Rationale ; gsndesign:rationaleText
    "GSN distinguishes rebut, undermine and undercut by what is targeted; ARGO distinguishes them by relation name, and has no relation that targets an inference. A sub-property of the undefined argo:opposes extends ARGO in the direction its authors left open, rather than redefining a term they had committed to."@en .

gsnaligndesign:why-0015 a gsndesign:Rationale ; gsndesign:rationaleText
    "The GSN standard is explicit that a solution makes no claim and is stated as a noun phrase. An ARGO premise must be affirmed, and affirming requires believing something true or false, which a noun phrase cannot be. Bridging the gap means minting the claim the evidence implicitly asserts - a claim the case author never wrote. For a nominal alignment that is not worth the authoring cost, and inventing claims on an author's behalf is the wrong default in an assurance case."@en .

gsnaligndesign:why-0016 a gsndesign:Rationale ; gsndesign:rationaleText
    "ARGO's requirements on arguments are existential restrictions, so under open-world semantics asserting that something is an argument entails that a creation act exists without requiring one to be named. Minting occurrent individuals would add four nodes per inference step to satisfy a requirement already satisfied. They become worth building only if the occurrent side is queried, or if case-content provenance is ever wired to PROV-O."@en .

gsnaligndesign:why-0017 a gsndesign:Rationale ; gsndesign:rationaleText
    "ARGO argues against attaching truth values to information entities and recommends an Act of Evaluation process instead. There is consequently no ARGO term for OntoGSN's boolean decorators to map onto, and reproducing ARGO's recommended shape means a process individual, an input and a nominal measurement for every flag on every element. Out of proportion to a nominal alignment; the boolean layer is simply omitted."@en .

gsnaligndesign:why-0018 a gsndesign:Rationale ; gsndesign:rationaleText
    "ARGO is deliberately small and scoped to arguments as such. Packaging, notation, modularity and evidence artefacts are outside it by design, so the absence of a counterpart is not a misalignment and inventing one would put terms in ARGO's namespace-space that its authors chose not to have."@en .

gsnaligndesign:why-0019 a gsndesign:Rationale ; gsndesign:rationaleText
    "ARGO mints complexargument at a hash IRI while every other term sits under its slash base. Writing the term against the declared base produces a different, undefined IRI and no error. Two prefixes make the discrepancy visible at the top of the file rather than hidden inside one axiom."@en .

gsnaligndesign:why-0020 a gsndesign:Rationale ; gsndesign:rationaleText
    "The derivation rules that populate the bridge classes are specified but not implemented. Writing them would mean a rules directory under alignments/, and the shape of the first rule - how a two-hop fan-in through a strategy collapses with a one-hop fan-in - is better settled against a fixture than guessed. The TBox is useful on its own as the statement of what the correspondence is."@en .


